              text: "# Climate Risk Assessment: 2025 Data Analysis\n\n## Summary of Key Indicators\n\n| Metric | Value | Benchmark | Status |\n|--------|-------|-----------|--------|\n| Temperature Rise | +1.3°C | 1.5°C Paris limit | ⚠️ Warning |\n| Arctic Ice Extent | Record Low | Historical baseline | 🔴 Critical |\n| Renewable Energy Share | 35% | 100% needed by ~2050 | ⚠️ Progressing |\n| CO2 Emissions Change | -2% YoY | -7-10%/year needed | 🔴 Insufficient |\n| Atmospheric CO2 | 425 ppm | 350 ppm safe threshold | 🔴 Critical |\n\n---\n\n## Risk Categories\n\n### 🔴 HIGH RISK\n\n**Atmospheric CO2 (425 ppm)**\n- Exceeds the widely cited 350 ppm safe boundary by 21%\n- Represents a **committed warming trajectory** regardless of near-term action\n- Risk: Continued warming even if emissions stopped today\n\n**Arctic Ice Loss**\n- Record lows accelerate albedo feedback loops\n- Risk: Self-reinforcing warming cycle becomes harder to interrupt\n- Secondary risks: Sea level rise, disrupted weather patterns, ecosystem collapse\n\n**Emissions Reduction Rate**\n- 2% annual reduction vs. 7-10% needed per IPCC pathways\n- Risk: Paris Agreement targets become mathematically unreachable\n- Current pace suggests **significant overshoot** of 1.5°C and possibly 2°C\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ MODERATE RISK / CAUTIONARY\n\n**Temperature at 1.3°C**\n- Only **0.2°C margin** remaining before the 1.5°C threshold\n- At current trajectory, 1.5°C likely crossed **within 5-10 years**\n- Risk: Tipping points may activate before targets are met\n\n**Renewable Energy Growth**\n- Positive trajectory (+6 percentage points in 2 years)\n- However, absolute speed remains insufficient\n- Risk: Growth may not offset rising global energy demand\n\n---\n\n## Key Risk Interactions\n\n```\nRecord Arctic Melt → Albedo Loss → Accelerated Warming\n        ↓\nCO2 at 425ppm → Committed warming → Harder emissions targets\n        ↓\n2% reduction rate → Gap widens → 1.5°C threshold approached rapidly\n```\n\nThe risks are **compounding and non-linear**, meaning individual metrics understate the combined trajectory.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Uncertainties\n\n- **Tipping points** (permafrost methane release, ice sheet collapse) could dramatically accelerate timelines\n- Renewable growth rate may **accelerate non-linearly** as costs fall\n- The 2% emissions reduction may reflect **structural change or temporary factors** — trend confirmation needed\n- Demand-side growth could **negate supply-side gains** in renewables\n\n---\n\n## Overall Risk Rating\n\n> ### 🔴 HIGH — Trajectory Concerning Despite Partial Progress\n\n**Rationale:** Positive signals (renewable growth, modest emissions reduction) exist but are materially insufficient relative to the scale and speed of change already embedded in the climate system. The margin for error before irreversible thresholds are crossed is narrow and shrinking.\n\n---\n\n*Note: This assessment is based solely on the provided data points. A complete assessment would incorporate regional breakdowns, socioeconomic vulnerability, and ecosystem-specific analysis.*",
