                  snippet:
                    "Emissions of the planet’s major greenhouse gases—carbon dioxide, methane, and nitrous oxide— continue to rise to record levels. CO2 atmospheric concentration reached 425.6±0.1 ppm in 2025, up 2.9 ppm from 2024, contributing to a cumulative ...\nAtmospheric carbon dioxide reached 425.6 parts per million in 2025, 53% above pre-industrial levels, while ocean heat and global sea level also set new records. The ocean has now broken its previous heat record for several years in a row. Emissions of the planet’s major greenhouse gases—carbon dioxide, methane, and nitrous oxide— continue to rise to record levels. CO2 atmospheric concentration reached 425.6±0.1 ppm in 2025, up 2.9 ppm from 2024, contributing to a cumulative 53% increase from the pre-industrial level of ~278 ppm.\nThe Arctic is also experiencing increased greenness; 2025 is the third-highest year for plant growth, while the Greenland Ice Sheet lost an estimated 129±50 Gigatons of ice overall for the year. Sea ice extent and thickness in the Arctic are also declining, while older ice is disappearing.\nThe sequence of the State of the Climate in 2025 is similar to previous years: Chapter 1—Introduction (i.e., this chapter); Chapter 2—Global Climate; Chapter 3—Global Oceans; Chapter 4—The Tropics; Chapter 5—The Arctic; Chapter 6—Antarctica and the Southern Ocean; and Chapter 7—Regional Climates, which covers the seven regions of North America, Central America and the Caribbean, South America, Africa, Europe and the Middle East, Asia, and Oceania.\nAbout 41% of the loss glaciers have experienced since 1976 has occurred in the last decade. Lakes in the Northern Hemisphere have also shown later ice formation and earlier ice breakup, with ice durations 15.0 days shorter on average than the 1991–2020 mean.",
